I check Google Business Profile and Search Console data for local clients across Reading and [Berkshire](/services/seo-berkshire/) most weeks, and I run 44 of my own sites, so I see local search behaviour from both sides of the screen. This page pulls together the local SEO statistics that are genuinely current for 2026, sourced from BrightLocal, Backlinko, Shopify, Whitespark, and Ahrefs, and flags the ones that get recycled every year without a fresh source behind them. It also covers what local SEOs are actually debating right now on forums such as Local Search Forum: whether replying to reviews affects rankings, why a rank tracker can disagree with what you see standing in your own shop, and what AI Overviews have done to the value of a Local Pack win.

## Why This Page Looks Different From the 2016 and 2017 Roundups Still Ranking

A lot of the local search statistics results still online date from 2016 or 2017. Google keeps surfacing this page for people searching that older version of the query. AI Overviews did not exist in 2016 or 2017 and now shape a meaningful share of local search results. Google Business Profile benchmarking has matured from headline multipliers into usable monthly averages (around 1,260 views and 33 website clicks) that a business can check its own profile against. Whitespark surveys practitioner opinions about ranking factors. Ahrefs found lower organic CTR in its informational-keyword samples; that does not establish a local-search-specific reduction. This page is not making a blanket claim that every underlying data point is from 2026. A handful of individual data points cite an older underlying baseline even inside a current study. They are flagged where they appear. The 46% local-intent estimate traces to 2018 (below). The mobile load-time abandonment figure in the AI Overviews section is an older finding still being recirculated without a fresh study behind it. The GBP interaction benchmark above comes from BrightLocal's 2019 study, and the Local Pack click-share breakdown from its 2018 click study. The consumer-behaviour research on star ratings and purchase likelihood is from 2017.

### Which 2016 to 2018 Local Search Statistics Still Hold Up?

Check an older statistic against this table before reusing it. Each row is discussed further down this page.

| Figure still circulating | Origin | Status on this page |
|---|---|---|
| 46% of Google searches have local intent | 2018 estimate, recycled since | Reported from a 2018 Google presentation; primary methodology unavailable. Do not treat 51% as an equivalent estimate |
| 53% of mobile users leave a page that takes over three seconds to load | Google, 2016 | Kept and flagged; no newer study found |
| 1.5 billion monthly "near me" searches | No 2026 source found | Unverified and excluded. No comparable primary-source volume verified |
| 136% "near me" growth since 2018 | No primary source found | Not used |
| Purchase likelihood peaks at a 4.0 to 4.7 star rating | Spiegel Research Center, 2017 | Kept as an ecommerce product conversion signal, not a ranking factor |
| 1,260 monthly views per Google Business Profile | BrightLocal, 2019 study of 2017-2018 data | Historical benchmark from 2017 to 2018 listing data; not a current performance target |

## How Much of Search Actually Has Local Intent?

**46%** of all Google searches have local intent. This is the most repeated figure in local SEO. Its origin matters: the estimate traces back to a 2018 Google-adjacent figure that almost every local SEO roundup since has recycled without an updated source, including the previous version of this page. Treat it as a rough proxy for "local search is a large minority of all search," not a precise 2026 measurement (BrightLocal, Local SEO Statistics, citing a Google presentation reported by Search Engine Roundtable, 2018). A 51% figure also circulates, but do not treat it as an equivalent estimate.

More current numbers back the same conclusion from different angles. **84%** of consumers searched for a local business in the past three months, and **73%** of local searches start on a mobile device (BrightLocal, Consumer Search Behavior 2026). **97%** of people check a business's online presence, its Google Business Profile, website, or reviews, before visiting or buying (Shopify, 2026).

For a [Reading](/services/seo-consultant-reading/) or Wokingham business, the practical read is simple. Whoever shows up complete and current when someone checks online wins the visit, regardless of how the 46% figure gets sliced.

