## "Near Me" Search Volume: The Sources Don't Agree, and That's Worth Saying Outright

Published "near me" search volume estimates disagree by a wide margin. This matters because the previous version of this page repeated a number I can no longer verify. No comparable primary-source monthly volume could be verified. The **1.5 billion** figure this page previously cited couldn't be matched against any 2026 source. Growth estimates are just as loose. Searchlab cites Google Trends data showing roughly **400%** growth in "near me" searches since 2020, and separately says they continue to rise by about **35%** a year (Searchlab, March 2026). Searchlab doesn't publish the underlying Google Trends series behind either number, so this page uses the cumulative 400% figure only and drops the annual rate as unverifiable.

Those numbers disagree because they're measuring different things: raw search volume against trend growth, over different date ranges, using different definitions of a "near me" query. A **136%** since-2018 growth figure also circulates widely, but I couldn't trace it to a primary source that still publishes it, so it gets the same scepticism as the rest: a plausible-sounding number nobody can currently stand behind. What's consistent across every source that does show its working is the direction. "Near me" search interest has climbed steadily since 2020 and shows no sign of reversing, even as AI Overviews absorb more of the resulting clicks. If a stats page hands you one precise "near me" search volume with total confidence, ask what it's actually counting before you build a strategy around it.

## Google Local Pack Statistics: Click-Through Rate by Position

The Local Pack (the three-listing panel with the map, shown above organic results) still decides most local search outcomes. Roughly **42%** of people who run a local search click on a result inside the Google Maps pack (Backlinko, updated December 2025). No current primary source for click-through rate by individual Local Pack position could be verified, so this page no longer quotes per-position CTRs. The most specific public breakdown is older and measures share of clicks instead, but it shows how steeply position matters inside the pack:

| Local Pack Position | Share of all results-page clicks (no Local Services Ads) |
|---|---|
| Position 1 | **17.3%** |
| Position 2 | **12.0%** |
| Position 3 | **2.9%** |

(Source: BrightLocal Local Services Ads click study, around 5,500 testers, San Francisco, October 2018)

For organic click-through rate by position, see my [Google CTR study](/blog/google-ctr-study/).

Note that these are shares of total clicks, each position's clicks divided by all clicks on the results page, not click-through rates, which divide clicks by impressions for one position. In that study the three pack positions together took **32.3%** of all clicks when no Local Services Ads were shown. The separate 42% figure above measures something else again: the share of local searchers who click somewhere in the Maps pack, from a different source and method. Fourth place in the Local Pack functions as no place at all, competing with page-two organic for scraps. Every part of a local SEO strategy, including [Google Business Profile optimisation](/services/local-seo/), on-page SEO, and review management, should be judged by whether it moves a business toward one of those three positions, not by whether it "helps local visibility" in the abstract.

Map Pack competition has intensified in Reading, Slough, and Bracknell specifically, as more local businesses invest properly in their profiles.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Local Pack clicks" title="Share of all results-page clicks by Local Pack position" data="Position 1:17.3|Position 2:12.0|Position 3:2.9" suffix="%" highlight="Position 1" source="BrightLocal Local Services Ads click study, 2018 (no LSAs shown)" /%}

## Google Business Profile Statistics: Clicks, Views, and What "Complete" Actually Buys You

Customers are **2.7 times** more likely to consider a business reputable if they find a complete Business Profile on Google Search and Maps, **70%** more likely to visit it, and **50%** more likely to consider buying from it (Google Business Profile Help). The widely repeated "7x more clicks" figure has no primary Google source I could find, so it's no longer used here. The most detailed public breakdown of what a typical profile generates comes from BrightLocal's Google My Business Insights Study: the average profile gets around **1,260 views** and **33 website clicks** a month, with **59 total actions** (website clicks, calls, and direction requests combined). That study is older than the rest of this page's sourcing, published in 2019 from data covering September 2017 to December 2018, but it's still the most current publicly available benchmark of its kind and a usable reference to check your own GBP Insights against, rather than only a headline multiplier with nothing to compare it to.

Even well-resourced brands leave gaps: SOCi's Local Visibility Index puts average profile completeness across multi-location brand locations at **86.6%**, even though **97.9%** have claimed their profiles (SOCi, 2026). Categories, services, a complete description, current opening hours including bank holidays, photos, and Q&A are the checklist. None of it is complicated. Most of it is unfinished on most profiles I audit.

