## Reviews and Local Pack Rankings: What the Data Shows, and Where Practitioners Disagree

**97%** of consumers read reviews for local businesses, and **49%** trust online reviews about as much as a personal recommendation (BrightLocal Local Consumer Review Survey 2026, US adults, published February 2026). Businesses in the top three spots in local search results average **561 Google reviews** each (Semrush, 2025, cited by Shopify, 2026). A widely repeated claim that businesses with 50 or more reviews are 266% more likely to appear in the Local Pack has no primary source I could find, so it's not used here. Review count matters, but chasing hundreds more matters less than most review-management vendors claim.

Star rating has a ceiling most people get backwards, though this next part is about conversion, not the ranking algorithm. The most-cited research here is a Spiegel Research Center (Northwestern University) study of ecommerce purchase behaviour, from 2017 but still the reference point nearly every 2026 local-SEO source borrows: purchase likelihood peaked with an average rating between **4.0 and 4.7**, then dropped slightly as ratings approached a perfect 5.0, because a spotless rating reads as fake to buyers (Spiegel Research Center, Northwestern, 2017). It was never a study of Local Pack ranking, and I haven't found a local-search-specific replication of it, but the underlying consumer-trust logic (a flawless rating looks curated) plausibly carries over to whether someone calls a business once they've found it. Treat it as a conversion signal to watch, not a ranking lever.

Review signals have grown from roughly **16%** to **20%** of Local Pack ranking weight since 2023, and recency now sits close behind raw count: in the 2026 scores, review quantity ranked ninth and review recency eleventh among Local Pack factors, and several contributors said a steady trickle of recent reviews can lift a business above one sitting on a larger but ageing pile. That comes from Whitespark's Local Search Ranking Factors survey (2026 edition, published November 2025, two years after the 2023 edition), one of the few sources that tracks change between editions rather than restating the same figure every January (Whitespark, 2026).

Replying to reviews is not itself a ranking factor, according to the consensus among experienced local SEOs on Local Search Forum. Nobody in that discussion could point to evidence that reply activity moves rankings directly, a live debate most stats pages skip past by asserting an answer without checking it first. What it does do is convert: **80%** of consumers say they're likely to use a business that responds to all its reviews, and **42%** say they're unlikely to use one that never replies (BrightLocal Local Consumer Review Survey 2026, US adults, published February 2026). Reply because it wins the customer reading it, not because you're chasing an algorithmic boost the evidence doesn't support (Local Search Forum, 2026).

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Review replies" title="How review reply habits affect consumers' choice" data="Likely to use if it replies to all reviews:80|Unlikely to use if it never replies:42" suffix="%" highlight="Likely to use if it replies to all reviews" source="BrightLocal Local Consumer Review Survey 2026 (US)" /%}

Competitor profiles sometimes carry a high "Local Guide" level with almost no visible review history, a gaming pattern flagged in threads on that same forum. Local Guide status can be built largely through photo uploads rather than genuine reviews, so a high guide level next to a thin review count isn't proof of a fake profile on its own. It's worth checking before assuming a competitor has earned more reviews than you through better follow-up alone (Local Search Forum, 2026).

## Why Your Own Search Doesn't Always Match What a Rank Tracker Shows

A business owner in a Local Search Forum thread searched for their own service standing inside their own shop and found themselves on page four of Google Maps, while a professional grid-based rank tracker showed them ranking first for the same term at the same location. Both readings were technically correct, and the mismatch comes up constantly with local business owners checking their own visibility.

Two things explain the gap. First, personalisation: if you or your staff search your own business repeatedly without clicking through to a competitor, Google can start treating that competitor's result as more relevant to you specifically. That's the opposite of what you'd expect. Second, location signal noise: a phone blends GPS, wifi, cell-tower triangulation, and IP address to guess where you are, and none of that is as precise or as controllable as the fixed coordinates a grid-tracking tool uses (Local Search Forum, 2026).

The practical takeaway: if you want to know where your business actually ranks, use a grid-based local rank tracker, or check from a clean, logged-out browser. Don't trust your own phone with years of search history attached to it.

## Mobile and AI Overviews: How People Actually Search Local Now

**73%** of local searches now start on mobile, and mobile local behaviour still punishes slow sites hard: **53%** of mobile users abandon a page that takes longer than three seconds to load (Google, "Find Out How You Stack Up to New Industry Benchmarks for Mobile Page Speed", 2016). That finding is a decade old, one of the oldest data points on this page, but it hasn't stopped catching out Berkshire businesses running older WordPress builds with unoptimised images.

What has changed is what happens after someone reaches the results page. AI Overviews appeared on **68%** of local business-type queries in a Whitespark study of six industries across Houston, Phoenix and Denver, though that average mixes local, informational and hybrid queries: pure local-intent queries such as "personal injury lawyers in Phoenix" showed one only about **15%** of the time (Whitespark, May 2025, cited by Shopify, 2026). A growing minority skip search results entirely: **45%** of consumers use ChatGPT or other generative AI tools for local business recommendations (BrightLocal Local Consumer Review Survey 2026, US adults, published February 2026). Ahrefs' updated study of AI Overview impact found a **58%** lower average click-through rate for the top-ranking page when an AI Overview is present, comparing December 2023 with December 2025 across 300,000 keywords, up from **34.5%** in its April 2025 study (Ahrefs, February 2026).

A Local Pack listing sits outside that organic snippet, which is one reason GBP visibility matters more now, not less. Content built to answer a question directly, not to rank for a keyword alone, is increasingly what gets cited when an AI Overview does the answering instead of the click. A [site built for local search](/website-design/small-business/) still outperforms a patched legacy build on mobile, independent of ranking position or AI citation.

## What This Means If You're Running a Local Business in Berkshire

Prioritise in this order, based on where the evidence and the return are clearest:

1. **Finish the Google Business Profile.** Customers are 2.7 times more likely to see a complete profile as reputable, and most profiles I audit are still unfinished, which makes this the highest-return hour available to most local businesses.
2. **Fix review recency, not review count alone.** A steady trickle beats a stalled pile. Don't chase a perfect 5.0. Reply to reviews for the customer reading them, not for a ranking effect the evidence doesn't support.
3. **Get mobile speed under three seconds.** More than half of local traffic is mobile, and more than half of that will leave a slow page before it converts.
4. **Check rankings with a grid tool, not your own phone.** Personalisation and location noise will lie to you.
5. **Write for the question, not the keyword alone.** AI Overviews are taking a growing share of organic clicks. Being the cited source matters as much as holding position one now.

